WEBVTT

NOTE Original editorial English translations. Latin passages quoted from Seneca Epistulae morales 26.

1
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.825
Tacitus reports that Seneca asked for the writing tablets of his will.

2
00:00:21.125 --> 00:00:29.976
The centurion refused. He left his friends only what remained: the image of his life.

3
00:00:35.576 --> 00:00:45.445
I told you I was within sight of old age. Now I fear that old age is already behind me.

4
00:00:48.745 --> 00:00:56.042
Years have marked my body. Yet I do not feel their injury in my mind.

5
00:00:59.442 --> 00:01:04.085
Each day takes something away from our strength.

6
00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:13.777
Is there a better end than slowly to dissolve, as nature releases us?

7
00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:26.868
I watch myself, as though the day has come which must judge all the years of my life.

8
00:01:30.268 --> 00:01:34.931
I will entrust to death the measure of my progress.

9
00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:45.253
That day will reveal whether I only speak with courage, or truly feel it.

10
00:01:49.853 --> 00:01:56.505
Put aside the opinions of other people. They are always uncertain.

11
00:01:59.405 --> 00:02:07.257
Put aside the studies pursued throughout your life. Death will pronounce the judgment.

12
00:02:10.557 --> 00:02:18.950
Learned conversation and the sayings of wise men do not reveal the true strength of the soul.

13
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.935
Even a frightened man may speak bravely.

14
00:02:29.335 --> 00:02:34.762
What you have done will be clear when you draw your last breath.

15
00:02:41.062 --> 00:02:46.615
Accipio condicionem. Non reformido iudicium.
I accept the condition. I do not fear the judgment.

16
00:02:53.915 --> 00:03:01.482
I say these things to myself. But take them as spoken to you as well.

17
00:03:04.582 --> 00:03:11.640
You are younger. What difference does it make? Years are not counted.

18
00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:26.274
Incertum est quo loco te mors exspectet. Itaque tu illam omni loco exspecta.
You cannot know where death awaits you; therefore be ready for it everywhere.

19
00:03:32.974 --> 00:03:37.675
To meditate on death is to meditate on freedom.

20
00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:47.420
Whoever has learned how to die has unlearned how to be a slave.

21
00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:57.505
There is only one chain that binds us: the love of life.

22
00:04:00.605 --> 00:04:10.677
We need not cast it away. Only loosen its hold, so that when necessity comes, we are ready.

23
00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:19.136
Vale.
Farewell.
